			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> I’d like to believe that. (This is why <a href=\"https://web.dev/learn/privacy/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"\">web.dev/learn/privacy/</span><span class=\"invisible\"></span></a> bangs on repeatedly about being transparent, because it’s a competitive advantage which your dishonest competitors cannot match.) But an actual misunderstanding of consent (on tech issues, _not_ on personal ones) is relatively easily mended; the repeated failure to do this obvious mending even after being told does suggest that it was malicious from the outset in many cases.</p>",
